# Pyson project context and agent handoff

Last verified: 2026-10-10. This is a Markdown-formatted handoff despite its
`.MF` extension. Read it before planning or changing this project. It records
project intent, current implementation, user preferences, and navigation to
the detailed references. It is not a replacement for reading relevant code.
Update it when architecture, public APIs, workflow, or accepted priorities change.

## 1. Purpose and product philosophy

Pyson is a Python-like compiled language for Game Boy and Game Boy Color games.
The name reflects Python and Jison. Source files currently use `.pysn`; do not
rename their extension merely because the project was renamed to Pyson.

The workflow is source project -> RGBDS assembly -> assembled, linked, and fixed
Game Boy ROM. Pyson is not CPython, an interpreter, or a Python runtime embedded
in a cartridge. Games execute generated native Game Boy instructions.

The central user requirement is:

> Pyson users should need to learn game development, without also needing to
> learn assembly to use its features.

Python familiarity plus game-development fundamentals should be sufficient for
advanced features. Beginners should be able to make basic games without complex
configuration. Familiar concepts include loops, states, cameras, animation,
graphics buffers, collision, and shared state. Keep syntax familiar and APIs
straightforward. Hide registers, interrupts, bank switching, and hardware timing
inside the compiler/runtime whenever practical. Expert manual optimization can
be added later; requiring assembly knowledge is not a current product goal.

The project is genre-neutral. RPGs were only an example, not the defining target.
The ideal ranges from Flappy Bird-like games to substantial games within actual
GB/GBC hardware constraints. Do not promise unlimited Python or modern-console
capabilities. Feature-rich does not mean finished, exhaustively optimized, or
certified on every physical device.

Tutorial positioning: prior Python knowledge is helpful, not a prerequisite.
Describe Pyson as its own language modeled closely on Python syntax and
conventions where practical, not as Python itself. Beginners can use widely
available Python tutorials to learn variables, conditions, loops, and functions,
then use Pyson's lessons/references for its supported features and differences.

## 2. Collaboration and authorization

- When the user is providing context and asks only for acknowledgement, respond
  that you understand. Summarize only when requested. Do not infer code-change
  authorization from context, a hypothetical question, or a compatibility check.
- The user's original workflow is description -> requested plan -> approved
  implementation. Later explicit instructions such as "go for it" authorize
  the discussed work; honor the scope of that authorization.
- During feature development the user prefers one feature at a time, with a
  compiled example they can test in an emulator before approving continuation.
  They have granted autonomy for specific batches before; that is not permanent
  authorization for every future feature or unrelated refactor.
- Explain behavior, practical benefits, and limitations plainly. Use meaningful
  progress updates during longer work and report what was actually tested.
- Preserve established examples and APIs where possible. Do not quietly migrate
  existing games to new timing or performance modes.
- All example projects, including Void Engine, are editable learning material.
  The earlier request not to change Void Engine meant creating a separate new
  demo rather than rewriting a demo the user liked. It was not a permanent
  protection rule or a restriction on learners. Preserve that distinction when
  making new demos; ordinary authorized example improvements remain allowed.
- The user wants a clean project suitable for a future public Git repository
  that others can clone and set up. Windows x64 is the supported automated
  development environment for now. Do not expand platform scope unsolicited.
- After compilation/testing, clean generated temporary build/test artifacts
  when the work is complete. Keep example source, assets, fixtures, tests, and
  relevant example ROMs for emulator testing. Never delete personal saves or
  accepted videos as incidental cleanup.
- Documentation and tutorials need to make advanced features approachable.
  During rendering work the user allowed full tutorials to wait, but requested
  that documentation needs be tracked. See the rendering notes backlog.

These are project preferences, not permission to override current user requests
or higher-priority environment instructions. Do not fabricate approvals.

## 3. Environment and reproducible commands

Current workspace: `C:\Users\Madz\Projects\GB_Python`, PowerShell on Windows.
The historical folder name does not change the product name. Avoid hardcoding
this machine's absolute paths into portable compiler or example code.

Pinned tools:

- Node.js >=18, 64-bit, with npm. Use `npm.cmd` in PowerShell.
- Jison 0.4.18, installed by `npm.cmd ci` using `package-lock.json`.
- RGBDS 1.0.4, installed project-locally in `tools/rgbds/`.
- Optional Python 3.10-3.13, 64-bit; Python 3.10 was verified here.
- Optional emulator dependencies: PyBoy 2.8.1, NumPy 2.2.6, PySDL2 0.9.17,
  and Windows pysdl2-dll 2.32.10, pinned in `tests/emulator-requirements.txt`.
- Optional FFmpeg for MP4 recording; not required to compile games.

Fresh-clone PowerShell workflow:

```powershell
npm.cmd ci
npm.cmd run setup
npm.cmd run doctor
npm.cmd run test:compiler
npm.cmd run build -- examples/hello-world/main.pysn hello-world
```

Optional runtime verification:

```powershell
npm.cmd run setup:emulator
npm.cmd run doctor -- --emulator
npm.cmd run test:emulator
```

Useful commands:

```powershell
npm.cmd test
npm.cmd run test:timing
npm.cmd run test:void-engine
npm.cmd run test:lucid
npm.cmd run test:profile
npm.cmd run test:tables
npm.cmd run test:examples
npm.cmd run test:toolchain
npm.cmd run build -- path/to/main.pysn output-name
npm.cmd run build:asm -- path/to/source.asm output-name
npm.cmd run profile -- examples/void-engine/main.pysn --target cgb --frames 600
```

Pass the output name explicitly: the CLI default is `pyson`, not the input
folder's name. `npm.cmd test` skips emulator cases when their environment is
absent; `test:emulator` requires it. A green run with skipped cases is not full
runtime verification. `test:compiler` still requires RGBDS for build checks.

Setup verifies the official toolchain download checksum. Its release metadata
is in `scripts/toolchain.json`. It does not require administrator rights, PATH
changes, or execution-policy changes. `PYSON_SETUP_PYTHON` selects setup Python;
`PYSON_EMULATOR_PYTHON` selects an existing emulator environment. Older `GBPY_`
aliases remain supported. See `documentation/development.md` for details.

Local sandbox execution may require tool approval for Node/npm. This is a session
environment constraint, not a requirement that users elevate PowerShell.

## 4. Repository navigation and compilation pipeline

| Location | Responsibility |
| --- | --- |
| `jison/language.jison` | Python-like grammar and AST construction. |
| `src/lexer.js` | Indentation-aware lexer; spaces, not tabs; bracket continuations. |
| `src/parser.js` | Loads Jison grammar and supplies the custom lexer. |
| `src/modules.js` | Resolves modules, namespaces, initialization, and source locations. |
| `src/language-extensions.js` | Preparation, constants, practical language extensions. |
| `src/compiler.js` | Compile entry point and header validation/orchestration. |
| `src/assets.js`, `src/world-assets.js` | BMP conversion, maps, palettes, tile budgets, streaming assets. |
| `src/constant-tables.js` | Immutable ROM-resident integer tables. |
| `src/codegen.js` | Types/scopes, definite assignment, expressions, statements, diagnostics. |
| `src/*-codegen.js` | Feature-specific builtin collection and emission. |
| `src/banking.js`, `src/code-banking.js` | Asset/function placement and bank-safe runtime wrappers. |
| `src/rom-template.js` | ROM layout, header, startup, runtime composition, data and WRAM. |
| `include/*.asm` | RGBDS runtime routines for graphics, language, sound, saves, etc. |
| `src/frame-codegen.js`, `include/frame-clock.asm` | Optional hardware frame timing. |
| `src/build-report.js` | Linked memory, banking, and diagnostics reports. |
| `src/cli.js`, `scripts/dev.js` | Compile/build CLI and RGBDS execution. |
| `scripts/environment.js`, `scripts/setup.js` | Windows tools and optional emulator setup. |
| `scripts/profile.js`, `scripts/profile.py` | Development profiling. |
| `examples/` | Source examples, included original assets, fixtures. |
| `dev-tools/` | Public optional MP4 recorders; local asset generators are ignored. |
| `documentation/` | Focused language/API references. |
| `tutorials/` | Eight progressive lessons linking explained source examples, build commands, and experiments. |
| `tests/` | Node tests and Python emulator checks. |

`compile(source, filename)` in `src/compiler.js` resolves modules, prepares the
AST, configures headers/saves/assets/tables, emits code, and plans banks. The
CLI writes assembly and metadata, then invokes `rgbasm`, `rgblink`, and `rgbfix`.
Parser files under `parser/` are generated/ignored, not the authoritative grammar.

Build outputs normally include:

- `build/<name>.asm`, object/linker/debug artifacts including `.sym` and `.map`.
- Asset, sound, music, animation, save, bank, and memory JSON manifests.
- `build/<name>.report.json`: linked ROM/WRAM budgets, addresses, banks, assets.
- `binary/<name>.gb`: playable ROM.

`build/`, `binary/`, downloaded tools, dependencies, saves, and state files are
ignored by Git. Keep reproducible source/assets in the repository. Existing
handwritten ASM fixtures may be required by tests; do not delete all `.asm`
files indiscriminately. `examples/toolchain-smoke.asm` is a required fixture.

## 5. Language semantics and constraints

Pyson deliberately implements a practical subset, not arbitrary Python.

- Indented `def`, `if/elif/else`, `while`, `for ... in range(...)`, `break`,
  `continue`, `pass`, and `return`; supported collections also have iteration.
- Signed 16-bit integers, -32768..32767. Arithmetic wraps in two's complement.
  Booleans are numeric 0/1. Decimal fractional literals use fixed-point Q12.4;
  no general floating-point or unlimited integers.
- Arithmetic `+ - * // %` and fixed-point `/`, bitwise `& | ^ ~`, signed comparisons and comparison
  chains, Python-like precedence and short-circuit `and`, `or`, `not`.
  Division floors; remainder follows the divisor's sign. See variables docs
  for overflow and division-by-zero behavior.
- Fixed types inferred from assignments and function calls. Literal ROM strings
  and the shared format_text buffer are referenced by pointers; strings are not
  general-purpose mutable Python objects.
- Definite-assignment checking: every read must be initialized on relevant paths.
- Functions are top-level, statically allocated, and automatically banked.
  Numeric arguments/results, string and integer-array parameters (RAM or ROM), record reference parameters, and pool handles are
  supported. Bare return/fallthrough returns 0. No recursion, including indirect.
- User function defaults are compile-time integers/fixed-point/booleans or constant
  expressions. Keyword arguments are supported; expressions evaluate once in
  written order, before overwriting static callee parameter storage.
- Parameters and assigned names are local unless declared `global`. Module
  initializers are required for globals. Qualified module writes update shared
  state without a `global` declaration.
- No closures, nested function definitions, first-class functions, classes,
  decorators, `nonlocal`, heap allocation, exceptions, or arbitrary Python
  library imports. No string returns or string defaults, dynamic dictionaries,
  resizable lists, or general slicing. Check focused docs before claiming an
  unsupported Python operation exists.

Math helpers include `abs`, `min`, `max`, `clamp`, and compile-time `constant`.
Formatting, seeded RNG, movement helpers, and runtime diagnostic messages also
exist; their contracts are in the focused docs below.

### Modules and storage

Imports resolve from the main source directory: `includes.state` identifies
`includes/state.pysn`. No package marker is needed. Asset paths instead resolve
relative to the file declaring the asset. Imports are top-level and static;
missing exports, circular dependencies, and conflicting bindings are errors.
`from module import name` currently imports functions; use qualified access for
shared variables/assets. Do not invent Python-style relative or dynamic imports.

Modules are compiled once per module name. Their initializer/storage/function
code is shared across importers, not duplicated for each screen. Dependency
initializers execute before dependent modules. Module variables belong to that
module, not automatically to one active screen or a runtime module object.

Numeric variables reserve two bytes at fixed WRAM addresses. Local variables
reserve memory per function; the CPU call stack is not a heap or local-variable
allocator. Repeated calls reuse that function's fixed locations. Same-named
locals in different functions do not automatically share storage.

Automatic lifetime-based local RAM reuse is explicitly deferred. Manual scratch
reuse uses a shared module with disposable variables. Prefer scratch use in
screen code. Do not use it in helpers unless callers, nested calls, and screens
are known not to clash. Document overwritten locations, initialize before use,
and never store persistent state there. This is a convention, not a checked
`volatile` variable feature. See `documentation/modules.md`.

### Collections

- Integer arrays are fixed-size, mutable WRAM storage, two bytes per element.
  They support indexing (including negative indices), `len`, iteration, and
  element updates under their documented bounds/type rules.
- `constant_table([...])` is a top-level readonly integer table in banked ROM.
  Reads, indexing, `len`, truth, and iteration resemble arrays. Tables can be
  passed to read-only array parameters; writes, aliases, and slicing are unsupported. A table contains
  at most 8191 elements and stays within one ROM bank. Reads restore bank state.
- Records provide fixed numeric field schemas; record arrays and helpers can
  reference original storage. They are not dynamic classes/dictionaries.
- Object pools provide fixed capacities, spawning/removal, validated handles,
  and active iteration. Handle identities and retirement behavior matter;
  consult `documentation/pools.md` before treating them as raw array indices.

## 6. What becomes ROM code versus compile-time data

Ordinary functions, loops, conditionals, assignments, and arithmetic produce
runtime assembly. The compiler does not generally execute game logic at build
time. A nested `for` loop calling `draw_map` remains a runtime drawing loop.

Header declarations, imports/namespaces, literal constants, BMP conversion,
static map layouts, animation definitions, and imported music definitions are
handled at compilation. Their results may be ROM data or generated instructions;
they do not require Python objects or a Python interpreter at runtime.

Nested lists supplied to static `tilemap`/`worldmap` constructors describe data.
They do not allocate runtime nested arrays or execute placement loops. Mutable
maps additionally allocate their documented working state. Read build reports
instead of guessing RAM/ROM costs from source length.

## 7. Graphics, assets, and screen updates

- Screen: 160x144 pixels; text uses an 8x8 font and 20x18 tile positions.
  `print(value, x, y[, palette])` clips and supports newlines; long lines do not
  automatically wrap. Font supports 95 printable ASCII characters.
- Drawing is buffered. `refresh_screen()` is the public update name. The older
  proposed `present()` name was intentionally replaced; do not revive it.
- `sprite_sheet(path, width, height)` imports BMP sprite frames; `sprite(slot,
  sheet, frame, x, y, flip_x=False, flip_y=False)` draws them. This API accepts
  5..7 arguments: there is no final per-instance palette argument.
- `tile(path, solid=False, terrain=0)` requires an 8x8 BMP. It does NOT accept a
  `palette=` argument. `tilemap` supports BMPs or nested tile rows. `mutable_map`
  supports editable nested maps. `worldmap` supports streamed larger worlds.
- `draw_map`, `draw_world`, `set_camera`, world sprites, map dimensions, tile
  queries, terrain/collision queries, rectangle hitboxes, and swept movement
  helpers are implemented. Ordinary map limits differ from streamed worlds.
- BMPs are converted into 2bpp tiles and deduplicated. Importing a full map does
  not reserve a new graphic tile for every repeated 8x8 region. Nested maps can
  still be useful for readable layouts and per-tile collision/terrain metadata.
- Four colors per tile. Imported background palette slots are 1..7; sprite
  palettes are 0..7. Palette allocation currently keys by source file and asset
  kind, not just identical RGB values. Reusing one file shares its palette;
  many distinct files can exhaust palette slots even with identical colors.
- `bg_palette(index,c0,c1,c2,c3)` and `sprite_palette(...)` set slots with RGB15
  integer literals or `"#RRGGBB"` strings. Screens may overwrite slots. Color
  conversion quantizes to hardware precision; DMG has monochrome behavior.
- Non-alpha sprite transparency uses the BMP's top-left color as transparent
  color 0; alpha BMPs use alpha transparency. Backgrounds are opaque. Color 0
  transparency is a sprite rule, not a universal background color key.
- Larger sprite frames consume multiple hardware sprites. Hardware totals are
  40 sprites and ten per scanline. Respect both when composing demos.
- Static resident font/assets share a 256-tile budget in the current renderer.
  Streamed worlds use a bounded cache in the remaining slots; large world
  graphics are not all resident at once. Check world-region budgets in docs and
  compile reports instead of assuming total world tiles must fit in VRAM.
- Rendering includes safe VRAM access, dirty rows, background map management,
  queued/gradual world loading, and buffered camera/sprite commits. Internal
  bulk world/window loads can pause the LCD. Smooth steady streaming is tested;
  it is not a universal zero-cost rendering guarantee.
- Window/HUD text, dialogue, typewriter pages, choices, dynamic number formatting,
  and sprite animation are implemented.

Asset format specifics and palette ordering are defined by `src/assets.js` and
`documentation/assets.md`. Do not infer them from a random external converter.

## 8. Timing, input, audio, and CPU speed

`wait_vblank()` waits for a fresh frame and samples buttons. `read_buttons()`
samples without waiting. `button`/`button_pressed` read that sample. A refresh
is not a substitute for explicit input sampling. Fast events between samples
may be missed. Source-end programs receive an automatic final refresh.

Default projects retain the historical cooperative wait-driven audio timing.
`wait_vblank` and internal refresh waits advance audio. A game update is not
necessarily one hardware frame. Adding both waits and refreshes can add delay.

Optional hardware timing, implemented most recently:

- `frame_count()` returns a VBlank count 0..32767, wrapping to zero.
- `elapsed_frames(previous)` computes the wrap-safe elapsed count.
- `wait_frames(count)` waits at least that many further display frames and then
  samples input; 0 performs no wait. Literal negative arguments are errors;
  runtime negatives yield zero/no wait.
- Using any helper in the source enables the clock at startup for that ROM.
  It allocates four WRAM bytes and enables an internal VBlank interrupt. Existing
  projects without helpers retain the default runtime and no clock allocation.
- The interrupt preserves AF, touches only the counter, and never switches
  banks or services audio. Timing-sensitive VRAM writes and OAM DMA are guarded.
- Audio controls synchronize after evaluating arguments and before changing
  playback. Waits service audio for elapsed display frames, keeping sequence
  position aligned under slower updates. Audio is still cooperative: a long
  computation can delay audible changes, and missed sounds cannot be recreated.
- The clock pauses while LCD is disabled. It is active display time, not wall
  time. Compare timestamps less than 32768 frames apart to avoid wrap ambiguity.
- Animation remains explicit: `update_animations(delta)` accepts 0..255 ticks.
  Bound updates or process longer deltas in chunks. For movement/collision,
  use small simulation steps when a large delta could jump through an obstacle.

Canonical elapsed-time pattern:

```python
previous = frame_count()
position = 0
while True:
    read_buttons()
    delta = elapsed_frames(previous)
    previous = (previous + delta) & 32767
    position = (position + delta) % 144
    sprite(0, hero, 0, position, 72)
    refresh_screen()
```

Advancing `previous` by the returned delta avoids losing the interval between
two independent reads. `hero` must be declared as a sprite sheet first.

`header.cpu_speed` defaults to `"normal"`; `"double"` is optional for `dual` or
`cgb` targets. Dual ROMs retain normal speed on monochrome hardware. This is a
startup runtime option, not an invented cartridge-header byte. It provides more
CPU work per display frame; update-count-based motion can therefore run faster.
Elapsed-frame movement avoids that dependence. Display timing and audio pitch
do not double. See `examples/cpu-speed` versus `examples/frame-timing`.

Music provides channel-1 pulse and channel-3 wave voices. JSON `music_file`
imports and literal `music_track` definitions are supported, including original
wave instruments, loops, restart, pause/resume, and controls. Effects use channel
2 pulse or channel 4 noise, with priorities and shared mute/volume controls.
Only used definitions and enabled feature runtimes are included. Tracker-file
imports and arbitrary audio formats are not implemented.

## 9. Banking, headers, saves, and diagnostics

Headers are literal main-module configuration before executable statements.
Unknown, duplicate, or incompatible settings are errors. Supported fields:
`title`, `target`, `controller`, `ram_kb`, `battery`, `rtc`, `rumble`, `sgb`,
`version`, `licensee`, `manufacturer`, `destination`, `rom_kb`, `cpu_speed`.
The source of defaults/validation is `src/compiler.js`; exact options are in
`documentation/header.md`. Logo, size bytes, and checksums are generated/fixed.

Targets: DMG, dual, Color-only. Controllers: ROM-only, MBC1, MBC3, MBC5. Banked
asset/function placement is automatic, respecting configured controller limits.
MBC5 supports the ninth ROM-bank bit. Main/runtime and small definitions remain
in ROM0, which is still a finite budget. A user function must fit within one
16 KB bank; split oversized functions. Do not assume everything can be moved
out of bank 0 or that cartridge capacity fixes a ROM0 overflow.

RTC, rumble, and SGB flags can be declared, but gameplay APIs for their hardware
operations are not implemented. Declaring a header capability is not proof of
runtime feature support.

Save APIs provide integer slots with defaults, committed-record validation,
immediate verified writes, existence, deletion, clearing, and capacity queries.
When omitted, save calls configure MBC5, 8 KB battery RAM. Configured controller
and RAM limits still apply. Never casually clear a user's save or change slot
ownership/schema; modules need agreed slot ranges. Physical EverDrive X7
compatibility was a design target, not a hardware certification from emulator
tests. Cartridge RAM is separate from the compiler's working RAM budget.

Runtime diagnostics include source context and errors such as zero division,
bad indices, and invalid handles. Build reports describe linked addresses and
memory budgets. Current allocator/report target is a 4096-byte working RAM
budget, not unrestricted use of all possible CGB memory banks.

The development profiler compiles a separate `profile-*` ROM and reports guest
CPU work/wait/idle, commit counts, refresh intervals, and source checkpoint
visits. It uses fresh in-memory saves. Defaults: 600 frames after 30 warmup.
Source visits are not per-line CPU costs; static frames without commits are not
necessarily missed deadlines. PyBoy source hooks above bank 255 are omitted and
reported. Tests compare profiler guest CPU timing and RAM against baseline.
One unattended profile does not cover interactive paths or certify hardware.

