| Example | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `hello-world` | Smallest approachable text example. |
| `star-chase` | Modular game/screens/shared state, colored assets and menus. |
| `vault-run`, `meteor-guard` | Larger gameplay combinations, collision/pools/saves. |
| `feature-lab` | Combined practical language/gameplay features. |
| `record-fleet`, `pool-shooter` | Records and managed fixed-capacity actors. |
| `dialogue-garden`, audio/animation galleries | Focused API examples. |
| `banked-world`, `banked-functions`, `streaming-world` | Banking and world streaming. |
| `cpu-speed` | Deliberate workload showing increased update rate at double speed. |
| `frame-timing` | Same travel pace under CPU load; hold A for heavy work. |
| `runtime-diagnostics` | Source-aware runtime error behavior. |
| `neon-requiem` | Four-scene Color demo, original dark synthwave/chiptune music. |
| `void-engine` | Six-scene dual-hardware demo and original music. |
| `lucid-circuit` | Separate dreamy four-world Color-only demo and original score. |
| `last-light` | Six-scene Lord of the Rings-inspired fantasy composition, dual hardware, original score, White Tree/Pyson finale. |
| `oath-of-stars` | Seven-act fantasy showcase: aurora/gate scenery, live orbital projection, birds, streamed worlds, phoenix/Pyson finale. |

Void Engine: normal CPU speed, dual target, MBC5+battery, 8 KB cartridge RAM,
128 KB ROM, 3027/4096 working RAM bytes used after ROM-table migration. Six
scenes include an opening, road, one-direction city flight, architecture,
projected particle torus, and a 40-sprite finale. The released ROM is distinct
from temporary double-speed/frame-clock regression variants.

Void Engine ROM SHA-256 at the recorded verification point (not an immutable target):
`9B0050C0CDD8DD317D62766540E2882109CCA0D3A39CCAF93F3BD9A12AA5E79D`.

Current `binary/void-engine.mp4` records a complete Color loop with audio,
approximately 106.718 seconds. User saves such as `binary/void-engine.sav` must
be preserved. Neon Requiem's accepted music/finale and existing preview video
also should not be replaced incidentally.

Lucid Circuit: Color-only, double CPU speed, standard MBC5 without save RAM,
1 MB ROM, four 512x512 abstract worlds, animated palettes, ROM motion tables,
24 geometric particles, and an original four-section two-voice score. Its
loop is approximately 91.834 seconds in the recorded emulator run. Its field
maps are static with palette/camera animation, not realtime 3D or raster plasma.
The full-loop test observed at most seven particles on a scanline.

The Last Light (2026-10-10) is a new, separate demo; established demos were not
rewritten. Build examples/last-light/main.pysn as last-light. It uses dual target,
CGB double speed with DMG normal-speed fallback, standard MBC5, six streamed
worlds, palette fades/cycles, perspective ROM poses, foreground parallax,
fractional camera movement, an animated eagle, and twelve original music parts
plus percussion. Each scene lasts 912 active display frames; the complete show
takes about 94 seconds including loads and loops without input. The final scene
is Pyson beneath the White Tree. Perspective is prepared geometry animated by
Pyson, not a general 3D/raster API. All BMP/JSON/Pyson assets are included.
Its local dev-tools/assets/last-light.js generator is ignored and never needed
by users or normal builds. npm.cmd run test:last-light builds and verifies a
complete loop on both emulated models, visible map cells/tile bytes, safe VRAM
writes, distinct scenes, audio, monotonic pans, and sprite scanline limits.
Observed maximum: seven sprites per scanline. Physical hardware is untested.
Final ROM: 256 KB, 3205/4096 WRAM bytes used, 891 free. The full runtime test
passed with 5583 CGB and 5616 DMG frames through one loop, including boot/load
time. All 30 example entry points compiled and 317 local links across the
feature docs, tutorials, and example READMEs resolved. Void Engine's recorded
ROM hash remained unchanged. Temporary build output was cleaned; the demo ROM,
source, assets, public preview, and repeatable test remain.
See examples/last-light/README.md and its preview.png for a source walkthrough.

Oath of Stars (2026-10-10) preserves The Last Light exactly. Build
examples/oath-of-stars/main.pysn as oath-of-stars. It is dual-compatible,
double-speed on CGB, normal-speed on DMG, MBC5 with 8 KB battery RAM. Seven
1024-active-frame acts use newly created assets/compositions and a new phoenix
Pyson finale. All data needed to compile is included. Its ignored local tool
is dev-tools/assets/oath-of-stars.js; normal users never need it.
Geometry rotates, morphs, and projects coordinates at runtime; six points
are updated per loop. A bounded 4097-word ROM lookup implements signed floor
division by 32 over -2048..2048, saving CPU work without allocating RAM.
Opening and gate use two areas of veil.bmp, a 96-pattern streamed world with
tile-aligned lettering. Palette shimmer and live projection supply movement;
the old progressively filled text fields were replaced, not optimized into
a new live-pixel renderer. Both backgrounds are complete from their first frame.
Comet record parameters update original records; a reusable pool supplies
particles. Rectangle collision, swept movement, and mutable rune tiles guide
their paths. Worlds stream, fractional cameras pan, two riders animate in
screen/world coordinates, sprites flip, and the sea scrolls vertically.
Two newly composed music voices per act each total 1024 ticks. Percussion and
palette cues share sixteen-frame subdivisions. Audio is cooperative. A skips
an act; Start pauses/resumes scene time and music. Completed/skipped-through
shows increment save slot 0, which the test validates after restarting.
This composition intentionally omits dialogue/choices/window buffers: they
would consume additional WRAM and change its graphics workflow.
Do not claim it invokes every built-in or implements filled-polygon/raster APIs.
It is more CPU-intensive than Last Light; frame-clock sequencing maintains act
and music timing but does not promise 60 visual updates per second.
npm.cmd run test:oath builds and verifies all acts on both emulated models,
visible map cells/tile bytes, safe streaming writes, sprite scanline limits,
audio, camera progression, input controls, persistent saves, and distinct
asset files relative to Last Light. Physical hardware remains untested.
Read examples/oath-of-stars/README.md for the source and performance walkthrough.
Visual refinement: box/triangle depth fragments became small points. Projection
remains in acts 0, 1, 3; act 4 uses subtle embers, act 5 keeps boat and birds,
and act 6 uses a sparse live star orbit. Original music and Last Light unchanged.
