## 11. Verification and cleanup practice

Latest completed full suite (2026-10-09, fixed-point numbers): **126 tests passed,
zero failures/skips**. Includes arithmetic and large intermediates, imports and
defaults, DMG/Color example movement/bouncing/A resets, and existing demos/games.
All 30 example entry points compiled and assembled; 345 local documentation links
resolved and all four complete fixed-point guide snippets compiled. Fractional
Motion's real ROM is binary/fractional-motion.gb. Generated build/test artifacts
were cleaned after verification. Void Engine source and ROM were unchanged by
this feature (SHA-256 2f76372a8b2de64aa4bb801d6c027fbdb1810812bf89efd597c6e95efbae05ac).

Previous completed full suite (2026-10-09, ROM-table parameters): **123 tests passed,
zero failures/skips**, including DMG/Color runtime checks for mixed RAM/ROM helpers,
MBC1/3/5 high-bank reads (including bank 257), forwarding, nested argument snapshots,
empty tables, bounds errors, and the Array Inventory reset controls. All 29 example
entry points compiled and assembled, and 327 local documentation/tutorial links
resolved. Array Inventory's real ROM was rebuilt. Generated build/test artifacts
were removed after verification. Existing demo source files were not edited.

Previous completed full suite (2026-10-09, mutable array parameters): 120 tests
passed, zero failures/skips. All 29 example entry points compiled and assembled;
327 local documentation/tutorial links resolved. After adding inference for
unused array helpers, all 94 compiler tests and 13 focused array/record/pool/string
checks also passed. Color Sprites' named calls
produce the same instructions as their positional equivalents. Void Engine's
ROM SHA-256 remained unchanged. The earlier frame-timing change also passed an
additional nine timing/audio tests after tightening synchronization around
argument evaluation. These are dated observations, not a guarantee for later
changes. Run checks appropriate to your changes.

New timing tests cover DMG, Color normal/double speed, rollover, deliberate CPU
load, music/wave/effects, animation, A-button behavior, window rendering, and
delayed argument evaluation. A separate clock-enabled Void Engine ROM passed
all six scenes on both models, including streaming data, legal VRAM writes,
scanline limits, motion, audio, and save/reload checks. Released demos were not
migrated to the new clock. Their normal regression cases also passed.

Node tests assemble ROMs and Python tests execute them in PyBoy, inspect actual
RAM/registers/maps/tile bytes, simulate input, and sometimes write PNGs. Pillow
is optional; the test harness can encode PNGs directly. Avoid tests that merely
mirror implementation; validate meaningful behavior and hardware constraints.
Do not claim physical hardware testing unless the user actually performed it.

For cleanup, verify resolved paths are inside this workspace before deleting
recursively. Use PowerShell `Remove-Item -LiteralPath` consistently. Remove
temporary contents of `build/` and known generated `runtime-*`/`profile-*` test
ROMs when finished, keeping real example ROMs. `runtime-diagnostics.gb` is a real
example despite its prefix. Preserve `.sav`, `.ram`, `.rtc`, state files, videos,
source tests, handwritten fixtures, and installed development tools unless the
user specifically authorizes their deletion. Build reports/symbols can be
regenerated; cleaning them means record/profile tools must rebuild first.

## 12. Remaining improvements, not implemented capabilities

The foundation is substantial enough to build games. Further work should focus
on measured performance, reliability, and accessible explanations, rather than
claiming every possible feature is complete. Potential priorities:

1. Faster graphics transfers using CGB DMA behind existing APIs.
2. More actionable performance diagnostics and transfer/frame budgets.
3. Optional approachable raster APIs for line scrolling/color effects.
4. Memory optimization, automatic local reuse if revisited, and broader CGB
   memory/VRAM-bank use without exposing mandatory assembly concepts.
5. Extend the implemented fixed-point scalar math if game examples demonstrate a need.
6. Tutorials progressing from tiny games to advanced rendering and simulation.
7. Stronger timeline/audio scheduling if cooperative servicing proves insufficient.

Do not implement these merely because they appear here. Discuss or proceed only
within the user's current authorization. The earlier abstract "Phase Shift"
concept is a proposal, not an implemented example or guaranteed roadmap item.
Existing asynchronous world rendering is real; a new generalized CGB DMA/raster
engine is not. Automatic lifetime-based RAM reuse remains in the icebox.

## 13. Detailed reference index and staleness rules

Read the relevant files before implementing a change or presenting exact API
syntax. References live in `documentation/`:

- Setup: `development.md`; cartridge configuration: `header.md`.
- Language: `variables.md`, `control-flow.md`, `functions.md`, `modules.md`,
  `arguments.md`, `math.md`, `arrays.md`, `constant-tables.md`, `records.md`, `pools.md`.
- Gameplay: `random.md`, `movement.md`, `collision.md`, `rectangles.md`,
  `tilemaps.md`, `mutable-maps.md`, `star-chase.md`, `feature-lab.md`.
- Graphics: `print.md`, `graphics.md`, `assets.md`, `scrolling.md`, `worlds.md`,
  `window.md`, `animation.md`, `dialogue.md`, `choices.md`.
- Timing/audio: `input.md`, `frame-timing.md`, `music.md`, `sound.md`.
- Tools/persistence: `banking.md`, `save.md`, `diagnostics.md`,
  `advanced-rendering-notes.md`.

The tutorial/documentation consistency pass corrected older statements about
interrupts, keyword arguments, types, streamed BMP limits, and text formatting.
`tutorials/README.md` is the learning-path index; `documentation/README.md` groups
the feature references. Rendering notes still contain historical measurements
and proposals, which must not be mistaken for supported APIs. The frame-timing
reference and current source define the optional clock's actual limitations.

If this handoff conflicts with current source/tests, investigate and update the
handoff; do not force the compiler to match a historical summary. Distinguish
implemented features, observed test outcomes, user preferences, and proposals.
Never infer a feature from its name, a header flag, or an impressive demo alone.

## 14. Consistent named built-in arguments

Fixed-parameter built-ins accept their public keyword names, normalized in
`src/builtin-arguments.js` at the beginning of `prepare()` before asset/audio
collectors inspect calls. `signatures` is the authoritative name/default catalog;
`documentation/arguments.md` lists it, and a test verifies catalog coverage.
Public names do not depend on internal ASM variables. Print uses `value`, palette
setters use `color0`..`color3`, and tilemap/worldmap use `source` (BMP or nested rows).
Unknown, duplicate, missing, excess, and positional-after-keyword arguments fail.
Existing positional calls remain supported. Defaults, types, literal-only options,
and hardware constraints retain their meanings. `randrange(stop=6)` starts at zero;
`play_sound(effect="collect")` retains the selected effect's priority.

Explicit expressions evaluate once in written order, including nested calls.
`argumentOrder` stores original slot order. Shared `evaluateArguments()` preserves
it while producing canonical stacked values for existing emitters. Reordered calls
use stack scratch rather than extra WRAM allocations; parameter-order calls retain
their existing assembly evaluation path. Dialogue and choices store directly by
source order. RGBDS stack-relative loads need two-byte instruction size accounting
in code-banking.js. Audio synchronization remains after evaluating arguments.

Tile `solid`/`terrain` and BMP world `collision` options remain keyword-only.
Collectors preserve absent options: noise must not acquire a pulse duty and pulse
music must not acquire a wave instrument. Record/records/pool/spawn field keywords
remain schema-driven; capacities and spawn pool remain positional. `format_text`
and `range` retain positional variable-length arguments. Math/movement helpers
continue through user-function keyword binding, with their existing publicParams.

Color Sprites now demonstrates mixed named calls without changing gameplay.
Tutorial 9 explains names versus variables, skipped defaults, and expression order;
tutorial 2 and the references link it. Regression tests cover public names, errors,
default gaps, exact assembly for parameter-order names, and DMG/CGB evaluation
order, nested calls, saves, collision, text, sprites, dialogue, choices, and audio.

## 15. User-function string parameters

User-defined functions now infer string parameters from all call sites, including
forward, named, nested, and imported helper calls. Type inference in records.js
retains `string` rather than treating all scalar arguments as integers. Codegen
passes text pointers through the existing two-byte static parameter slots, after
evaluating every argument in source order. There is no character copy, heap,
garbage collector, or separate string runtime. Existing record/handle inference,
fixed-type errors, definite initialization, and recursion rejection remain.

Accept literal strings, string variables (including module strings), and
format_text results. A parameter may forward text, alias it locally, rebind to
another string locally, print it, or start dialogue with it. It cannot alternate
between numeric values and strings. Numeric returns can be integers or fixed-point; string returns,
string defaults, concatenation, indexing, comparison, and mutation are unsupported.
Numeric defaults for other parameters still work normally.
Literal-only built-ins retain their restrictions; string parameters cannot serve
as format_text templates, dialogue_speaker names, or compile-time asset paths.

Formatted text refers to one shared buffer, not a snapshot. Another formatting
call overwrites aliases and parameters pointing to it. Consume it before another
formatting call; do not pass two separately formatted messages expecting independent
contents, or overwrite the buffer while dialogue still reads it. Literal ROM
strings are stable and deduplicated. See functions.md and tutorial 10.

Dialogue Garden's open_message(text, delay=2) demonstrates the feature with
existing imported messages; its controls and conversations remain the same.
String-parameter tests cover inferred types, forwarding, modules, invalid mixed
types, nested calls, formatted text, empty/multiline text, HUDs, and dialogue
on both emulator models. The standalone agent manual remains deferred until
the feature/API work settles; keep this handoff and focused documentation current.

## 16. Mutable integer-array parameters

Functions accept named integer arrays and read-only ROM tables by reference,
with inferred types and existing positional/named binding. RAM-only parameters
reserve four bytes: base address and actual length. ROM-capable parameters reserve
six bytes, including the bank. Owners still have fixed allocations; the same helper
can accept arrays of different lengths, including zero. No array elements are
copied at the call boundary. Forwarding and local arrays are supported while
their owner is active; arrays cannot be returned or persisted as ordinary aliases.

records.js marks inferred array parameters with arrayReference and bytes=4.
Indexing, len, and direct iteration also infer unused helper parameters so
utility modules do not need dummy calls solely to establish array types.
Codegen loads their base/length dynamically for len, truth, indexing, and direct
iteration. Calls snapshot pointer plus length on the stack in written argument
order, then populate parameter slots after all expressions finish. Nested calls
cannot overwrite a pending outer call's argument metadata. Parameter literals
are bounds-checked at runtime; owner literals still have compile-time checks.
Both ordinary and augmented writes update caller storage without global.

Rebinding a parameter to a list, another array, or an iteration value is rejected.
Record arrays, inline list expressions, and whole pools remain unsupported as
integer-array arguments. Read-only constant_table values are now supported for
parameters that do not write, including through other helpers. ROM table
reads/banking through module variables retain their existing behavior.
Two parameters may reference the same array, with writes visible through both.
Direct iteration copies integers into the loop variable rather than referring
to individual array cells. Function return values can be integers or fixed-point, not arrays.

Array Inventory now restores its initial quantities with copy_items(target,
source), demonstrating explicit element copies from a ROM table to RAM. Tutorial
11 and arrays.md explain reference semantics, lengths, bounds, mutability, and
ROM-table differences. Regression tests cover different/empty lengths, negative
indices, forwarding, imported helpers, local arrays, shared aliases, nested calls,
and actual runtime bounds errors on DMG and Color.

## 17. ROM tables as array parameters

src/array-parameters.js analyzes calls after ordinary type inference. ROM capability
propagates from table owners through parameter forwarding. Write requirements
propagate in the reverse direction from indexed writes through all helper calls.
Passing a read-only table to a writing parameter fails at compile time, including
conditional/augmented/transitive writes. Requirements are per parameter, so a
copy helper can write its target while reading a ROM source. This conservative
analysis does not depend on branches running or being optimized away.

ROM-capable parameters have romReference=true and bytes=6: pointer at +0,
length at +2, 16-bit bank at +4. RAM-only parameters stay bytes=4. Bank $ffff
identifies RAM; legal ROM banks are 0..511. Calls snapshot the optional bank
alongside pointer/length before populating callee slots, preserving nested and
named evaluation order. Forwarding a RAM-only parameter to a ROM-capable helper
supplies the RAM sentinel. ReadArrayParameterWord dispatches direct RAM reads or
the existing ReadConstantTableWord, which executes in ROM0 and restores the
execution bank. No table elements are copied or allocated in WRAM at a call.

No new source syntax or annotations. Indexing, negative indices, len, iteration,
truth, varying/empty lengths, imports, and bounds diagnostics work for both storage
kinds. No array returns, rebinding, general aliases, slices, or dynamic allocation.
Documentation arrays/functions/constant-tables and tutorial 11 cover this feature.
constant-tables.md#passing-tables-to-functions provides a plain-language guide:
RAM/ROM meanings, when to choose each, a shared total helper, rejected writes,
copying ROM defaults to RAM, expected results, and restrictions. Keep feature
explanations approachable without requiring assembly or hardware-bank knowledge.

## 18. Fixed-point scalar numbers

Decimal literals and fixed(value) use signed Q12.4: two bytes, steps of 1/16,
range -2048..2047.9375. Literals round nearest, ties away from zero; runtime
products and fractional division truncate toward zero. Runtime overflow wraps
modulo 65536 like integers, including integer-to-fixed conversions; literals
outside the fixed range fail. int(value) truncates toward zero and accepts
integers unchanged. No general floating point or scientific notation.

Lexer/Jison add DECIMAL and `/`/`/=`. Decimal AST nodes have type fixed; prepare
accepts compile-time fractional defaults. Codegen type inference promotes scalar
variables/parameters to fixed if they receive fixed values anywhere, and promotes
a function's numeric returns if any return is fixed. Integer arguments and
assignments convert automatically in fixed contexts. String/record/handle/array
restrictions remain. Function forwarding, modules, named evaluation order,
default arguments, abs/min/max/clamp, comparisons/chains, truth and unary signs
work. +,-,* return fixed if either operand is fixed; / requires a fixed operand;
// returns a floor integer; % returns a remainder with the divisor's sign.
Bitwise operations and integer APIs require explicit int() conversion.
Integer-only programs retain their assembly/runtime behavior.

FixedMul and FixedDiv in include/fixed.asm use full unsigned 32-bit intermediates
and apply the signed result, avoiding premature 16-bit intermediate overflow.
FixedToInt handles negative truncation. Required runtime routines are included
only when graphics.fixed is enabled by conversions or multiply/divide/comparison;
eight shared scratch bytes are inside wStateStart..wStateEnd for accurate reports.
No per-operation heap allocation. Bounds/ranges of integer collections remain;
integer arrays, ROM tables, record/pool fields do not store fixed elements.
range bounds and loop counters remain integer-only. Out-of-range mixed runtime
conversions can wrap, so keep operands within the fixed range.

documentation/fixed-point.md is a plain-language guide with small complete
examples, conversion meanings, precision/limits, errors, and practical uses.
Tutorial 12 links examples/fractional-motion/main.pysn (dual, three bouncing
sprites at 1/0.5/0.125 pixels per game update, A resets). Counts are per update,
not a promise of one update per hardware frame. Existing examples were kept.
Tests cover signs, rounding, raw representation, large intermediates, helpers,
imports/defaults/nesting, integer-only compatibility, zero division diagnostics,
and example bouncing/reset on DMG and Color.
